    title: "Can the Doha Framework end the conflict in eastern DRC?",
    summary: "Kinshasa hands over 15 prisoners to powerful rebel alliance.",
    preview: "Can the Doha Framework end the conflict in eastern DRC? Stalled for months, the peace process in the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo appears to be gaining momentum. The Rwandan-backed M23 rebel group has accepted 15 prisoners released by the government in Kinshasa. The handover is part of a framework agreement signed last year, after talks mediated by Qatar.",
    body: "Can the Doha Framework end the conflict in eastern DRC?\n\nStalled for months, the peace process in the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo appears to be gaining momentum.\n\nThe Rwandan-backed M23 rebel group has accepted 15 prisoners released by the government in Kinshasa. The handover is part of a framework agreement signed last year, after talks mediated by Qatar.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 4 items- list 1 of 4DR Congo Ebola health workers protest unpaid wages as deaths surpass 1,800\n\n- list 2 of 4WHO chief: Ebola outbreak in DRC spreading at ‘unprecedented rate’\n\n- list 3 of 4DR Congo releases 15 prisoners to M23 rebels as part of Doha peace process\n\n- list 4 of 4‘I’ve never been counted’: DR Congo’s first census in 42 years\n\nIt may be a small step, but it could pave the way to ending the fighting in the eastern DRC. Previous attempts have failed to bring peace to the region.\n\nCould this mechanism address the core issues of the long-running conflict?\n\nPresenter: Per Nyberg\n\nGuests:\n\nKambale Musavuli – Analyst at the Center for Research on the Congo-Kinshasa\n\nFelix Ndahinda – Researcher on the Great Lakes region, specialising in conflict resolution, peace and transitional justice\n\nAbdulla Al Etaibi – Assistant Professor in International Affairs at Qatar University",
